Answer: According to the source, face mask mandates in San Francisco appeared to decreasemortality: "The order appeared to work in 'flattening the curve' of infection in San Francisco," and when the mandate was prematurely lifted at the end of November 1918, "the death toll rose again in a third wave of the disease," leading to the reintroduction of mandatory mask-wearing. This suggests that mask mandates were associated with reduced mortality, and their removal was followed by increased deaths.

